        "name": "Question 9",
        "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1$ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\infty $\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, and the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/51/515b4cbcb39ad80d4401adffd450aed9e222663c469f1cf1cf36cbd900b5eb4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
        "answer_fields_complete": true,
        "result": "Incorrect",
        "html": "<div id=\"question-143587\" class=\"question\"><div class=\"questionHeader\"><span class=\"questionNumber\">9.</span><span class=\"helpButton\" questionid=\"143587\">?</span></div><div class=\"questionText\"><p>Consider the polar curve <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"179\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45F TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c73\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c69\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c6E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2061\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D703 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2212\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo data-mjx-texclass=\"NONE\">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> for <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"180\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D703 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2208\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mrow space=\"4\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c5B\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mi