        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/observations/lesson-step-38606-491fce6c53a57c9d3f01.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/observations/lesson-step-38606-491fce6c53a57c9d3f01.png",
        "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/topics/3895",
        "captured_at": 1792064663.2284899,
        "math_academy_id": 6384,
        "topic_id": "3895",
        "sequence_position": null,
        "source_step": {
          "dom_id": "step-t6384",
          "placement_id": null,
          "content_id": null,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)",
        "title": "Using Limits to Find Horizontal Tangents"
      },
      {
        "dom_id": "step-37500",
        "name": "Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases",
        "problem": "Let's consider the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ for $0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/09b25bca42f62b6567cecf4b14987113003ccea6dd869a569be75af4fa1781c9.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00.\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve at this point. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/001914b359c9c95f6a8fceb655a5233242065eb4231765bcfc0d652ebbf4eed8.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines become steeper and steeper as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/40de3bbd7f8422ff4ab4af6091497579f8031b8a43296e466f8c23ed4a121097.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* as we approach $C,$ it would appear that these tangent lines become vertical. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/caf6255e2ae97865da52dec64413e2e61a9a807f50b54ae4eae2f2b96f030c30.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes vertical like this, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at $C.$",
        "worked_solution": "",
        "calculator_instructions": "",
        "fields": [],
        "problem_field_keys": [],
        "answer_fields_complete": null,
        "result": null,
        "proof_feedback": null,
        "html": "<div id=\"step-37500\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block\" stepid=\"37500\" steptype=\"tutorial\" contentid=\"6185\">\n            <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                <div class=\"stepName\">\n                    <a id=\"stepAnchor-37500\" class=\"stepAnchor\" name=\"step-6\">Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases</a>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"37500\">?</div>\n            </div> \n\n            <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage * {\n                            transition: none;\n                            -webkit-transition: none;\n                            -moz-transition: none;\n                            -ms-transition: none;\n                            -o-transition: none\n                            }\n\n                            .mjx-svg-href {\n                            fill: blue;\n                            stroke: blue\n                            }\n\n                            .MathJax_SVG_LineBox {\n                            display: table!important\n                            }\n\n                            .MathJax_SVG_LineBox span {\n                            display: table-cell!important;\n                            width: 10000em!important;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage__block {\n                            text-align: center;\n                            margin: 1em 0em;\n                            position: relative;\n                            display: block!important;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            width: 100%\n                            }</style><p>Let's consider the curve <span class=\"mjpage\"><svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"12.483ex\" height=\"2.343ex\" style=\"vertical-align: -0.505ex;\" viewBox=\"0 -791.3 5374.7 1008.6\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-labelledby=\"MathJax-SVG-1-Title\">\n<title id=\"MathJax-SVG-1-Title\">r = 1-\\sin\\theta</title>\n<defs aria-hidden=\"true\">\n<path stroke-width=\"1\" id=\"E1-MJMATHI-72\" d=\"M21 287Q22 290 23 295T28 317T38 348T53 381