          "content_id": null,
          "type": null
        },
        "content": "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines' slopes grow to infinity as we approach $\\theta = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta-\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=\\cos\\theta - \\sin2\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\n- Applying L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the left-sided limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the limit in the denominator, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^-} \\left(\\cos\\theta - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\\right) = 0^-,\n$$\n\nwhere the notation $0^-$ means that the denominator approaches zero from the negative numbers.  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Using similar methods, it can be shown that the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, we conclude that there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/ea/ea0a03e575d962bd8d69505e20166dcc60c77cfe933f30762bb81296ccf7a797.png)",
        "title": "Using Limits to Find Vertical Tangents"
      }
    ],
