          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/observations/step-t6303-45592f8a62b91013da79.html",
          "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/observations/step-t6303-45592f8a62b91013da79.png",
          "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14227801/topics/3895/lesson",
          "captured_at": 1792064334.7227433,
          "math_academy_id": "6303",
          "topic_id": "3895",
          "sequence_position": null,
          "source_step": {
            "dom_id": "step-t6303",
            "placement_id": null,
            "content_id": null,
            "type": null
          },
          "content": "Let's consider the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ for $0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/e72d6a7d4b490a37f99723d0cde12a378f038b43ba373547e9c8feaf71952717.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where $\\theta = \\pi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00.\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening at this point, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/d03ca27e8cecd32aebc0e6914ac2ab128bce57ab876b96e8aaa91b562041ba4c.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/4fda95ed912c83ac36ec772b6afa6554ad8a81b7c98a330fd774d501b4bbecdf.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* of the tangent lines as we approach $C,$ it would appear that they become horizontal. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/50f8bd688c55d8458a9db100469cd62138efc200402496d3adb18f90ea53e747.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes horizontal like this, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at $C.$",
          "title": "Introduction"
        },
        "capture_key": "step-t6303",
        "observed_position": 0
      },
