        "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point $\\theta_0$, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative $y'(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ into the expression for $y'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot (-1)}{-2\\cdot 0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/1bff05165761ba775dfae73cd4d41c28f5a939b8caca602d516636a6fb05ae75.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note**: We'll discuss vertical tangents in more detail later in this lesson.",
        "can_dont_know": false,
        "can_skip": false,
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-e10116",
          "name": "Example: Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases",
          "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $\\theta \\in \\left[0, 2\\pi\\right).$ Given that \n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) =  2\\cos2\\theta - 2\\sin\\theta, \\qquad\ny'(\\theta) = 2\\cos\\theta+2\\sin2\\theta,\n$$\n\nand $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $y'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right) = 0$\n\nII. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi/2} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
          "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point $\\theta_0$, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative $y'(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ into the expression for $y'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi ) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}}{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi /2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot (-1)}{-2\\cdot 0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/1bff05165761ba775dfae73cd4d41c28f5a939b8caca602d516636a6fb05ae75.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note**: We'll discuss vertical tangents in more detail later in this lesson.",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
          "fields": [],
          "problem_field_keys": [],
          "answer_fields_complete": null,
          "result": null,
          "proof_feedback": null,
          "html": "<div id=\"step-e10116\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block;\">\n    \n                 \n                <div class=\"example\">\n                    <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                        <div class=\"stepName\"><span class=\"stepType\">Example:</span> Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases</div>       \n                        <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"e10116\">?</div>\n                    </div>\n            \n                    <div class=\"exampleQuestion\">\n                        <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage * {\