              "captured_from": "step-t6185"
            },
            {
              "index": 3,
              "tag": "img",
              "html": "<img src=\"/graphics/ce96fa17a8b9cf883d8b168ad019b51f\">",
              "extraction_part": "problem",
              "dom_index": 3,
              "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/graphics/ce96fa17a8b9cf883d8b168ad019b51f",
              "render_ready": true,
              "path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/caf6255e2ae97865da52dec64413e2e61a9a807f50b54ae4eae2f2b96f030c30.png",
              "sha256": "caf6255e2ae97865da52dec64413e2e61a9a807f50b54ae4eae2f2b96f030c30",
              "content_type": "image/png",
              "representation": "original",
              "captured_from": "step-t6185"
            }
          ],
          "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/observations/step-t6185-0962738dec43ded75ae6.html",
          "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/observations/step-t6185-0962738dec43ded75ae6.png",
          "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14227801/topics/3895/lesson",
          "captured_at": 1792064453.8313177,
          "math_academy_id": "6185",
          "topic_id": "3895",
          "sequence_position": null,
          "source_step": {
            "dom_id": "step-t6185",
            "placement_id": null,
            "content_id": null,
            "type": null
          },
          "content": "Let's consider the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ for $0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/09b25bca42f62b6567cecf4b14987113003ccea6dd869a569be75af4fa1781c9.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00.\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve at this point. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/001914b359c9c95f6a8fceb655a5233242065eb4231765bcfc0d652ebbf4eed8.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines become steeper and steeper as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/40de3bbd7f8422ff4ab4af6091497579f8031b8a43296e466f8c23ed4a121097.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* as we approach $C,$ it would appear that these tangent lines become vertical. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/caf6255e2ae97865da52dec64413e2e61a9a807f50b54ae4eae2f2b96f030c30.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes vertical like this, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at $C.$",
          "title": "Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases"
        },
        "capture_key": "step-t6185",
        "observed_position": 8
      },
