          {
            "kind": "progress_document",
            "at": 1792064320.3410077,
            "url": "https://mathacademy.com/learn",
            "captured_at": 1792064320.3380177,
            "source_sha256": "5b0c1e546b405f4f0476918ff4d2aa63a13d47c0d142144cfa4c1ec5a5506ee3",
            "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/browser-originals/5b0c1e546b405f4f0476918ff4d2aa63a13d47c0d142144cfa4c1ec5a5506ee3.html",
            "status": 200,
            "headers": {
              "date": "Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:38:40 GMT",
              "etag": "W/\"3ab0d-74E+xYuhsediMyG7t99HK4s0QbM\""
            },
            "transformation": null,
            "rendered_sha256": "5b0c1e546b405f4f0476918ff4d2aa63a13d47c0d142144cfa4c1ec5a5506ee3"
          }
        ],
        "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/observations/step-e10244-f3bb4a54bf98d2081ef6.html",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/observations/step-e10244-f3bb4a54bf98d2081ef6.png",
        "worked_solution": "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $y'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (0+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
        "can_dont_know": false,
        "can_skip": false,
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-e10244",
          "name": "Example: Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases",
          "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r=\\cot^2\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in \\left(0, \\pi\\right).$ Given that \n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta(\\cos\\theta + 2\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta), \\qquad\ny'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta(1+\\sin^2\\theta),\n$$\n\nand $y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $x'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right) = 0$\n\nII. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\to {0}^{+}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{-} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\to {0}^{-}\\,\\text{as}\\,\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
          "worked_solution": "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that $y'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (0+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-\\cancel{cot}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
          "fields": [],
          "problem_field_keys": [],
          "answer_fields_complete": null,
          "result": null,
          "proof_feedback": null,
          "html": "<div id=\"step-e10244\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block;\">\n    \n                 \n                <div class=\"example\">\n                    <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                        <div class=\"stepName\"><span class=\"stepType\">Example:</span> Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases</div>       \n                        <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"e10244\">?</div>\n                    </div>\n            \n                    <div class=\"exampleQuestion\">\n                        <style type=\"text/css\">\n                            .mjpage .MJX-monospace {\n                            font-family: monospace\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage .MJX-sans-serif {\n                            font-family: sans-serif\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage {\n                            display: inline;\n                            font-style: normal;\n                            font-weight: normal;\n                            line-height: normal;\n                            font-size: 100%;\n                            font-size-adjust: none;\n                            text-indent: 0;\n                            text-align: left;\n                            text-transform: none;\n                            letter-spacing: normal;\n                            word-spacing: normal;\n                            word-wrap: normal;\n                            white-space: nowrap;\n                            float: none;\n                            direction: ltr;\n                            max-width: none;\n                            max-height: none;\n                            min-width: 0;\n                            min-height: 0;\n                            border: 0;\n                            padding: 0;\n                            margin: 0\n                            }\n\n                            .mjpage * {\n 