      {
        "key": "step-q143720",
        "question": {
          "dom_id": "step-q143720",
          "name": "Question 2",
          "problem": "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*",
          "worked_solution": "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14227801/assets/8b68110c25bfa464c0971a396ec50908c0c0622cbdc92bf42239b984695655ca.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
          "calculator_instructions": "",
          "fields": [
            {
              "key": "selection",
              "type": "radio",
              "choices": [
                {
                  "option": "a",
                  "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-143720-a",
                  "type": "text",
                  "value": "I only",
                  "html": "I only"
                },
                {
                  "option": "b",
                  "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-143720-b",
                  "type": "text",
                  "value": "II and III only",
                  "html": "II and III only"
                },
                {
                  "option": "c",
                  "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-143720-c",
                  "type": "text",
                  "value": "None of the statements are true",
                  "html": " None of the statements are true"
                },
                {
                  "option": "d",
                  "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-143720-d",
                  "type": "text",
                  "value": "I, II, and III",
                  "html": "I, II, and III"
                },
                {
                  "option": "e",
                  "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-143720-e",
                  "type": "text",
                  "value": "I and II only",
                  "html": "I and II only"
                }
              ],
              "choices_complete": true
            }
          ],
          "problem_field_keys": [],
          "answer_fields_complete": true,
          "result": "Correct",
          "proof_feedback": null,
          "html": "<div id=\"step-q143720\" class=\"step questionWidget\" style=\"display: block;\"><div class=\"questionWidget-header\"><div class=\"questionWidget-title\">Question 2</div><div class=\"questionWidget-helpButton\">?</div></div><div class=\"questionWidget-text\"><p>Consider the polar curve <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"38\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45F TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-msup space=\"4\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c63\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c6F\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c73\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.363em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c33\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2061\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D703 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo data-mjx-texclass=\"NONE\">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> for <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"39\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D703 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2208\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mrow space=\"4\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c5B\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\