        "solution": {
          "status": "ambiguous",
          "reasoning": "I is true because x'(0)=\\sin0\\cos0=0. For nearby nonzero \\theta, dy/dx=\\sin\\theta(3\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)/(2\\cos\\theta\\cos(2\\theta)), which tends to 0 as \\theta\\to0. Thus II is true, and the curve has a horizontal tangent at \\theta=0, so III is true. The supplied worked solution concerns a different question and does not establish Math Academy's current answer.",
          "confidence": 1,
          "answers": [
            {
              "key": "selection",
              "correct_value": "I, II, and III",
              "correct_option": "d",
              "value_type": "text",
              "wrong_value": "I only",
              "correct_keys": [],
              "wrong_keys": [],
              "ma_value": null,
              "ma_basis": "none",
              "ma_evidence": "",
