        "solution": {
          "status": "ambiguous",
          "reasoning": "I is true: y'(\\pi/2)=-\\cos(\\pi/2)+\\sin\\pi=0. Applying L'Hopital's rule gives the slope limit through (\\sin\\theta+2\\cos(2\\theta))/(\\cos\\theta-2\\sin(2\\theta)). Its numerator tends to -1, while the denominator approaches zero negatively from the left and positively from the right. Thus the left slope limit is +\\infty and the right is -\\infty. II and III are both true. The supplied worked solution concerns a different curve; Math Academy's current answer is not captured.",
          "confidence": 1,
          "answers": [
            {
              "key": "selection",
              "correct_value": "I, II, and III",
              "correct_option": "d",
              "value_type": "text",
              "wrong_value": "I only",
